---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { howToSchema } from "~/lib/structured-data";
import { SITE_URL } from "~/lib/og";
import {
  INSTALL_CMD,
  PHANTOM_BREW,
  PHANTOM_NPM,
  PUBLISHED_VERSION,
  SOURCE_VERSION,
} from "~/lib/site-facts.generated";

const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/docs/quickstart/`;

const howTo = howToSchema({
  name: "Install Ashlr Stack and provision Supabase",
  description:
    "Install the tools, initialize a project, attempt one provider setup and inspect its results.",
  url: pageUrl,
  steps: [
    {
      name: "Install Stack",
      text: "Install the @ashlr/stack CLI and its required runtime; check the installed version and help.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#1-install-stack`,
    },
    {
      name: "Verify the install",
      text: "Run `stack --version` and `stack status` to inspect the installed version and local metadata.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#2-verify-the-install`,
    },
    {
      name: "Initialise a project",
      text: "Run `stack init --noInteractive` in the intended project to write blank split config; inspect the files and ignore entry.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#3-initialise-a-project`,
    },
    {
      name: "Add your first service",
      text: "Authorize `stack add supabase` for provider setup and credential/MCP/config writes; inspect failures and partial effects.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#4-add-your-first-service`,
    },
    {
      name: "Sanity-check with doctor",
      text: "Run `stack doctor` to inspect implemented adapter checks and services without a check.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#5-sanity-check-with-doctor`,
    },
    {
      name: "Forward your app command to Phantom",
      text: "Use `stack exec -- bun dev` to forward startup to installed Phantom exec; verify app inputs separately.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#6-run-your-app-with-secrets-injected`,
    },
  ],
});
---

<Docs
  title="Quickstart"
  description="Install, initialize, attempt one provider setup and inspect the app and MCP configuration."
  eyebrow="Getting started"
  extraSchemas={[howTo]}
>
  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>
    Stack is a thin orchestration layer. Runtime requirements depend on how you
    install it, and secret operations use a separate tool:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/docs/phantom/">Phantom Secrets</a></strong> — handles credential persistence.
      Real <code>stack add</code> needs its supported project setup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Node.js 20+</strong> — the published 0.3.0 CLI and MCP packages declare this
      minimum. Check the manifests for other versions. Recorded public packages are
      <strong>{PUBLISHED_VERSION}</strong>; reviewed source <strong>{SOURCE_VERSION}</strong>
      and native release binaries are separate artifacts. Source development uses Bun.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Install Stack</h2>
  <p>One-liner:</p>

  <Code
    code={INSTALL_CMD}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>Or via your package manager:</p>

  <Code
    code={`npm i -g @ashlr/stack@${PUBLISHED_VERSION}
# or use Bun as the package manager
bun add -g @ashlr/stack@${PUBLISHED_VERSION}`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>And Phantom (required for anything that touches secrets):</p>

  <Code
    code={`${PHANTOM_BREW}
# or
${PHANTOM_NPM}`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2>2. Verify the install</h2>

  <Code
    code={`stack --version
stack --help
stack status`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>Illustrative local status; inspect your actual report:</p>

  <Code
    code={`  Ashlr Stack

  ● .stack.toml       missing — run \`stack init\`
  ● phantom           0 secrets in vault
  ● services          0 configured`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Status reports local metadata and slot presence. An indicator alone
    does not prove provider access or usable application credentials.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Initialise a project</h2>
  <p>Inside any repo (Next.js, Astro, Rails, whatever):</p>

  <Code
    code={`cd path/to/your-app
stack init --noInteractive`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    This writes a blank config; add services one at a time. Interactive
    <code>stack init</code> offers a template picker. A selected template
    runs provider setup by default unless <code>--noProvision</code> is supplied.
    Illustrative interactive output:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`┌  stack init
│
◇  Pick a starter template (or blank):
│  Blank — add services later with \`stack add\`
│
└  Wrote .stack.toml (stk_8f3c6a2e).`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>Two files appear on disk:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.stack.toml</code> — committed. The <em>shape</em> of your stack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.stack.local.toml</code> — gitignored automatically. Your
      project_id and per-developer instance data.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Stack attempts to append <code>.stack.local.toml</code> to your <code>.gitignore</code>.
    Writes can partially fail; inspect files before sharing. See the <a href="/docs/config/">config
    reference</a> for the full schema.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Add your first service</h2>
  <p>Let's wire up Supabase — the most interesting one, because it has a full
    upstream provisioning flow plus an MCP server.</p>

  <Code
    code={`stack add supabase`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>For authorized Supabase setup, the adapter attempts:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Authenticate using the available Supabase OAuth or PAT branch.</li>
    <li>Create a project in an accessible organization, or attach to a requested existing resource.</li>
    <li>Handle resource-critical credentials through managed persistence phases.</li>
    <li>Fetch the anon + service-role keys and route them into Phantom.</li>
    <li>Write an available <code>supabase</code> MCP descriptor to <code>.mcp.json</code>; client startup and credential setup remain separate.</li>
    <li>Write the service into <code>.stack.toml</code> + <code>.stack.local.toml</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Illustrative successful output, not a provider acceptance receipt:</p>

  <Code
    code={`┌  stack add supabase
│
◇  ● supabase (abcdefghijklmnop) · 3 secrets · mcp wired
│
└  ✓ supabase is live.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Review the persistence result.</strong> Phantom can refuse setup,
    existing names or cleanup. Stack handles values in memory and may retain
    partial effects; local files are not a general redaction boundary. See <a
    href="/docs/phantom/">Phantom</a> for the full story.
  </div>

  <h2>5. Sanity-check with <code>doctor</code></h2>
  <p>
    Before you move on, run <code>stack doctor</code> and inspect the checks
    it implements. Some adapters have no health check; a successful report
    does not prove every provider or app works:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`stack doctor`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <Code
    code={`┌  stack doctor
│
◇  ● supabase (illustrative check result)
│
└  All services healthy.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Run this before deploys, after a stale laptop comes out of a drawer, or any
    time something feels off. In CI, use <code>stack doctor --json</code> —
    exit code <code>0</code> if no reported errors, <code>1</code> for reported failures.
    Unchecked services still need separate verification.
    See <a href="/docs/ci/">CI integration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="6-run-your-app-with-secrets-injected">6. Forward your app command to Phantom</h2>
  <p>
    Instead of <code>bun dev</code>, run <code>stack exec -- bun dev</code>.
    The wrapper forwards the command to installed Phantom. Configure the
    app's required inputs and verify them separately:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`stack exec -- bun dev`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Your app may need <code>SUPABASE_URL</code>, <code>SUPABASE_ANON_KEY</code>,
    and <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code>. Stack does not automatically
    write populated env files or guarantee those values reach the app.
    Use <code>stack env export --example</code> for an empty-key skeleton.
  </p>

  <h2>What now?</h2>

  <div class="not-prose mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <a href="/docs/cli/" class="docs-card group">
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white mb-1 group-hover:text-magenta-200 transition-colors">
        CLI reference
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-400 leading-relaxed">
        Every command, flag, and default. Bookmark-worthy.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/providers/" class="docs-card group">
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white mb-1 group-hover:text-magenta-200 transition-colors">
        Providers
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-400 leading-relaxed">
        The 39 curated providers — what each wires and where its dashboard is.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/templates/" class="docs-card group">
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white mb-1 group-hover:text-magenta-200 transition-colors">
        Templates
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-400 leading-relaxed">
        Five opinionated starter stacks — the "SaaS in a box" ones.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/mcp/" class="docs-card group">
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-white mb-1 group-hover:text-magenta-200 transition-colors">
        MCP in Claude Code
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-400 leading-relaxed">
        Drive Stack from Claude / Cursor / Windsurf via MCP tools.
      </p>
    </a>
  </div>
</Docs>
